+// A C++ narrow string literal carries its own length, but `flight::String`'s `const char*` constructor
+// measures its argument with `strlen`. A source string containing U+0000 is therefore truncated at it
+// silently -- the emitted text compiles and the program reads a different string than the source
+// declared, which for a key built by joining two fields means two distinct keys become one. A value that
+// contains U+0000 is built from the literal's own extent instead; `sizeof` measures the array including
+// its terminator, so the constructor receives every byte the literal holds. Every other value keeps the
+// plain form, byte for byte, so nothing else in the emitted tree changes. This is the third site that
+// builds a Flight string from a source literal, and the one the measured defect goes through: a composite
+// map key is written as a template literal, whose text chunks were built with JSON.stringify directly.
